# How much each buffer holds, in the layer's own unit. One row per (filing, layer, buffer), and
# `sized` says whether that row carries a number or a typed reason it does not.
#
# Amounts, not yes or no
#   A yes-or-no says a buffer exists, not how much it holds, so any share at all would fit inside
#   it and the rule that bounds shares by slack could never fail. Three rows carrying amounts make
#   the bound checkable.
#
# An unsized row is still a row
#   And `none` is not one of the reasons it can carry. The capacity and inventory slacks are
#   `pm:StatedClaim` and the time slack is `pm:StatedTimeSlack`, and all three take their blank
#   through a `pm:ClaimAbsence`, so a buffer somebody looked at and found empty is a sized row
#   reading 0, 0 and 0 in the layer's unit. That leaves `unmeasured`, nobody looked, and
#   `notApplicable`, the buffer does not arise here, for `absent`; a time slack may instead name
#   `clearance` in `derivation`, for the receiver to work out. Filling an unsized row with 0.0
#   destroys the difference for good, and it is the difference between a bound that holds and a
#   bound nobody tested.
-- pm:Layer/pm:timeSlack with pm:Nameplate/pm:capacitySlack and pm:inventorySlack;
-- the element names are the kinds.
SELECT s.filing, s.layer, s.buffer,
       s.low, s.mode, s.high, s.unit, s.absent,
       (s.low IS NOT NULL) AS sized,
       s.derivation
FROM pm.slack s
